./docs/reports/integrated_engine_provisional_human_explanation_guide_usage_trial_round2_v0.md:114:The protected boundary is that material must keep its authority state while it moves. Gemini can provide visual and conceptual material. Codex can translate that material against the current integrated-engine baseline, classify what is usable, what must be held, what can be carried forward, and what conflicts with current core. The user controls whether a later package opens for implementation, promotion, external harvest, or final wording.
./docs/reports/integrated_engine_provisional_human_explanation_guide_usage_trial_round2_v0.md:116:Workspace ownership belongs in this boundary layer. `gemini/` material has proposal authority. `docs/reports/` can contain Codex-written canonical trial records when the user opens a documentation package. `runtime/views` and `runtime/manifests` remain closed here. This is not folder responsibility; it is artifact authority and collision prevention.
./docs/reports/integrated_engine_provisional_human_explanation_guide_usage_trial_round2_v0.md:122:Gemini mock elements that suggest supervisor queues, watcher recommendations, live manifest truth, bridge panels, line atlas as VectorFL center, team/role console dominance, or engine control-room authority remain outside current core. Some may be `carry-forward` material for later reread. Some are `reject / conflict` for the current baseline because they would cross protected surface, authority, or runtime boundaries.
./docs/reports/integrated_engine_provisional_human_explanation_guide_usage_trial_round2_v0.md:126:A later route can open only through a scoped user decision. If the user opens a package, Codex can translate the relevant material again under that package's boundary. The result may still be reference-only, `hold`, `carry-forward`, `reject / conflict`, or `needs user decision`; translation does not equal promotion.
